 "source_type": "instagram",
 "source_url": "https://www.instagram.com/reel/Dd1cArnOU0B/",
 "title": "Last-Minute Interview Preparation",
 "topic": "communication",
 "summary": "This post provides a quick 20-minute preparation guide for a last-minute job interview. The creator shares a simple process covering company values, self-introduction, and smart questions to ask.",
 "key_points": [
  "Go to the company's career page and pick some corporate values that align with you to use when asked why you want to work there.",
  "Prepare a 'tell me about yourself' answer in 1 to 2 sentences describing who you are, what you currently do, and what you want, keeping it under 2 minutes.",
  "Review the job description to see what skills and responsibilities are listed, and have a couple of examples ready that align with them.",
  "Ask these three questions at the end of the interview: 1. What would make someone exceptional/great at this role? 2. What would you want me to accomplish in my first six months? 3. What does performance development management look like?",
  "Record yourself on your phone answering interview questions to check for confidence, filler words, nervousness, or talking too much."
 ],
 "content": "A last-minute interview? Don’t panic. All you need is 20 minutes. #career #interviewprep #worklife Oh f**k your interview is today and you've prepared f**k all. It's fine. As a recruiter, this is exactly what I would do. First, I want you going to that company's career page and just pick some corporate bullshit that aligns with you. So when they ask you, 'Why do you want to work here?', that's your answer. Next, prepare your 'tell me about yourself' answer. Super simple. I want you giving one to two sentences to describe who you are, what you currently do, and what you want, and keep it under two minutes. Then go to the job description and see what skills and responsibilities they've listed. Boom, those are the questions they're going to ask you in the interview, so just have a couple of examples ready to go aligned to that. Then, at the end of the interview, you're going to ask these questions: 1. What would make someone exceptional/great at this role? 2. What would you want me to accomplish in my first six months? And 3. What does performance development management look like in this role? But I've interviewed thousands of people and let me tell you, what you say actually matters less than how you say it sometimes. Literally pick up your phone and record yourself answering interview questions so you can watch it back and see if you're speaking confidently. Are you using filler words? Do you sound super nervous? Are you talking too much? And that's literally it. I am so sorry, it should not take hours to prepare for interviews. I like it quick and dirty. You procrastinated, but they'll never know. Happy job hunting! Important text on screen: ARITZIA, New Clothing Outerwear Dresses Sweatsfleece Accessories Discover, Our Values, Who We Are is reflected in our values., These are the central and key beliefs that guide our actions, unite our People and uphold our tenets of Everyday Luxury., Who you are, What you currently do, What you want, Keep it under 2 mins, Questions to ask:, 1. What would make someone exceptional/great at this role?, 2. What would you want me to accomplish in my first six months?, 3. What does performance development management look like?, Happy job hunting!",
